{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on ac1bfc63: adopt the distinction. A full ReadMinimum strictly below the journal floor is satisfied for ordering only; at or above the floor it still requires the exact retained hash.\n\n1. Below the floor, after authority and the ScopeRef and incarnation checks, entry answers archived with the floor, commit and through position, as main does today; summary, items, history and projection answer normally once the current head has passed the position. The supplied historical hash is not asserted against archived bytes, and the answer grants no access and no archive dereference. The result carries no new field for this; the archived answer's existing metadata is the record. Name the case in docs/kernel.md section 7 in one sentence: below the floor the minimum orders, it does not verify.\n\n2. At or above the floor, a full minimum whose retained hash differs or is missing stays unavailable, as adoption dc7be8f4 says.\n\n3. followReceipt keeps its documented refusals hash-mismatch and reference-mismatch. Your plan, a qualified sequence barrier plus the handle's own receipt checks after the read, is the right shape; the README, the handle comment and the hosted route then stay as they are, and the 409 refusal on the hosted enrolment read-back is preserved. The single-file exception is withdrawn, as you say; the final head runs one full gate.\n\n4. Cases: one native case with a real floor covering followReceipt and a card refresh below the floor; one for a full minimum above the floor with a wrong hash; the checker's items 2, 3 and 5 each with their case.\n\nNon-blocking point 1 of the checker's report, no cap on waiters, is deferred: the request adds no rate policy, and the one-second bound limits the hold; record it in the note as a known bound and I will carry it to the observability design.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ac1bfc630c840df26f083f436ae1600eea85ff8c